𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟭𝟭 𝗼𝗳 𝟯𝟭: 𝗚𝗿𝗮𝗽𝗵𝗤𝗟

REST gives you fixed endpoints — one URL per resource, one shape per response. GraphQL flips that: one endpoint, and the client describes exactly which fields it wants in a single query.

𝗘𝘃𝗲𝗿𝘆 𝗿𝗲𝗾𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁 𝘀𝗵𝗮𝗽𝗲𝘀 𝗶𝘁𝘀 𝗼𝘄𝗻 𝗿𝗲𝘀𝗽𝗼𝗻𝘀𝗲:

🟢 query { user{ name } } → fetch just the name
🔵 query { user{ posts{title} } } → fetch just the posts
🟠 mutation { updateUser(id) } → update a user
🔴 subscription { newMessage } → live updates

𝗥𝗲𝗮𝗹-𝘄𝗼𝗿𝗹𝗱 𝗲𝘅𝗮𝗺𝗽𝗹𝗲: a mobile app needs "user + last 3 posts". With REST that's two calls stitched together on the client. With GraphQL it's one round-trip, returning exactly this:

{ user(id: 123) {
name
posts(limit: 3) { title }
} }

𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝘆𝗼𝘂 𝗴𝗮𝗶𝗻:
✅ No over-fetching or under-fetching — clients ask for exactly what they need
✅ Fewer round-trips — big win on slow or mobile networks
✅ One schema — self-documenting, strongly typed contract between client and server

𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗶𝘁 𝗰𝗼𝘀𝘁𝘀:
⚠️ Harder to cache — no fixed URL per response, so CDN/HTTP caching needs extra work
⚠️ Server complexity — resolvers, N+1 query problems, and query-depth limits to guard against abuse
⚠️ Steeper learning curve for teams used to REST conventions

